    "elevatorPitch": "Traditional decision theory demands spreadsheets and decision trees, but real combat and crisis commanders execute winning moves in seconds. Gary Klein reveals the tactical engine of true expertise: pattern recognition, mental simulation, and decisive execution under high-stakes uncertainty.",
    "executiveSummary": "Classroom management models assume decision-makers have unlimited time, neat probability trees, and structured data. Real-world execution is the exact opposite: fog of war, shifting goalposts, ticking clocks, and brutal consequences. When a seasoned plant manager senses an imminent equipment explosion or a lead surgeon alters an incision mid-procedure, they do not weigh options in a matrix.\n\nInstead, their accumulated cognitive repertoire flags subtle anomalies, selects a battle-tested blueprint, and deploys it immediately.\n\nKlein terms this engine the Recognition-Primed Decision (RPD) model. Experts size up a landscape by categorizing it against thousands of historical hours. Rather than hunting for an elusive optimal choice, they evaluate their first intuitive response by running a rapid mental simulation.\n\nIf that simulation detects a fatal flaw, they tweak the play or drop it for the next intuitive match. This satisfies the iron rule of high-stakes environments: a timely, workable decision executed decisively always defeats an optimal choice arrived at too late.\n\nTrue strategic advantage stems from cultivating internal cognitive assets rather than relying on bloated analytical software. Leaders must understand operational intent, spot invisible baseline deviations, and communicate mission objectives clearly. By prioritizing experiential repertoire over algorithmic formulas, organizations convert ambiguous crisis into operational command.",
