      "headline": "The Recognition-Primed Decision Model",
      "keyInsight": "For decades, management theorists preached that intelligent people always compare multiple choices to find the best one. Gary Klein turned that doctrine upside down with the Recognition-Primed Decision (RPD) model.\n\nExperts do not waste energy generating mediocre backup plans. When confronted with a crisis, their brain immediately surfaces the single most viable action first.\n\nHow do they test it without comparing it to others? They run a rapid mental simulation.\n\nThey project the movie forward in their mind: 'If I deploy the crew to the eastern flank, will the pressure hold?' If the mental movie plays out cleanly, they pull the trigger. If they spot a fatal snag, they tweak the plan or discard it entirely and move to option two.\n\nThis is called satisficing\u2014finding the first move that works instead of hunting for the elusive perfect move. In competitive arenas where speed is life, committing to a good plan now beats drafting a perfect plan five minutes too late.",
      "content": "Picture a chess grandmaster walking past a park table. He glances at an active board for three seconds and calls out the winning move. He did not evaluate four hundred piece movements or calculate twenty turns ahead.\n\nHis experienced eye recognized the structural tension in the defense and instantly isolated the killer attack. The grandmaster does not browse through ten blunders before finding the masterstroke; his intuition delivers the right move first.\n\nThis is the core architecture of the Recognition-Primed Decision model. When experienced professionals encounter a critical problem, their memory matches the situation to an existing archetype. This archetype immediately tells them what goals are feasible, what cues matter, what will happen next, and what move to take.\n\nThere is no endless list of alternatives cluttering their mental workspace.\n\nOnce the primary move emerges, the operator does something brilliant: they mentally test-drive the action. They play a short mental video of the sequence unfolding against real-world friction. If the simulation reveals an obstacle, they patch the plan on the fly.\n\nIf it is fundamentally broken, they drop it and simulate the next plausible candidate. This singular focus preserves cognitive bandwidth and guarantees decisive, high-velocity execution.",
